\begin_body

\begin_layout Itemize
The Hamiltonian,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

,
 is an important operator because it appears in two essentially important equations from quantum mechanics:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The 
\series bold
time-dependent Schrödinger equation
\series default
 (TDSE),
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{dt}\ket{\psi}=-\frac{i}{\hbar}\hat{H}\ket{\psi}\label{eq:TDSE}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is an imaginary number and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hbar$
\end_inset

 is the 
\series bold
reduced Planck constant
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 is constant in time,
 i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $d\hat{H}/dt=0$
\end_inset

,
 then,
 given an initial state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi\left(0\right)}$
\end_inset

,
 it can be shown that the state at some later time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ket{\psi\left(t\right)}=\exp\left(-\frac{i}{\hbar}\hat{H}t\right)\ket{\psi\left(0\right)}.\label{eq:TDSE-Solution-Const-H}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This may often be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ket{\psi\left(t\right)}=\hat{U}\left(t\right)\ket{\psi\left(0\right)},\label{eq:TDSE-Solution-Const-H-compact}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{U}\left(t\right)=\exp\left(-\frac{i}{\hbar}\hat{H}t\right)\label{eq:TimeEvolutionOperator}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 is not constant,
 we need other methods to calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi\left(t\right)}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The 
\series bold
time-independent Schrödinger equation
\series default
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{H}\ket{\psi_{n}}=E_{n}\ket{\psi_{n}}\label{eq:TISE}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

also is a very important equation in quantum mechanics
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Solutions to the time-independent Schrödinger equation (TISE) involve finding the eigenenergies 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ E_{n}\right\} $
\end_inset

 and their associated eigenstates,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ket{\psi_{n}}\right\} .$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This is a direct application of linear algebra
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We often write the Hamiltonian as a matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Then,
 finding 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi_{n}}$
\end_inset

 and the corresponding 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{n}$
\end_inset

 is a matter of finding the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The quantum state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi_{n}}$
\end_inset

 is said to be the eigenstate associated with eigenenergy 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{n}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
These have a VERY important role to play in all of physics and chemistry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The eigenstates and eigenenergies are the reason electrons do not simply collapse to a point and rest against the nucleus of an atom:
 this state does not have an energy equal to an allowed energy,
 and thus,
 this state is forbidden!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The eigenstates of electrons in atoms determine how atoms interact with one another
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Without the eigenstates and eigenenergies from quantum mechanics and linear algebra,
 chemistry would be VERY boring,
 and biological life as we know it would be impossible,
 and the universe would be very uninteresting
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Warning:
 the Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 is not the same as the Hadamard transformation,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{H}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 is used to describe the energetics of a quantum system
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Hadamard 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{H}$
\end_inset

 is used in quantum information processing applications
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Pay close attention so you can distinguish when we are talking about a Hamiltonian or a Hadamard operation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For clarity,
 I will attempt to use two different symbols,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{H}$
\end_inset

,
 to help us distinguish between the Hadamard operation and the Hamiltonian
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We will focus on the simpler of the two equations,
 the time-independent Schrödinger equation of Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:TISE"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Notice that Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:TISE"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) is nothing more than an eigenvalue problem,
 with eigenvalues 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ E_{n}\right\} $
\end_inset

 and eigenstates 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ket{\psi_{n}}\right\} $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In the context of quantum mechanics,
 the eigenvalues 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ E_{n}\right\} $
\end_inset

 are given the name 
\series bold
eigenenergies
\series default
,
 since they are energies in fact
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 the eigenenergies have units of energy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The eigenenergies of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 specify the only values that can be obtained on a measurement of the total system energy
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Because of this,
 the eigenenergies may be thought of as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

allowed energies
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Other energies can never be measured and are said to be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

forbidden energies
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Quantum states 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ket{\psi_{n}}\right\} $
\end_inset

 are unitless,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{n}$
\end_inset

 has units of energy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 must also have units of energy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The electron-volt:
 a Unit of Energy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Electron-volts are more appropriate for quantum systems than Joules
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Recall the Volt is an energy per unit charge:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
1\;\text{V}=\frac{1\;\text{J}}{1\;\text{C}}\label{eq:defineVolt}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The electron-volt (eV) is an energy per unit charge times a chage,
 which is an energy:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1\;\text{eV}=\frac{1\;\text{J}}{1\;\text{C}}q_{e},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{e}$
\end_inset

 is the fundamental charge:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q_{e}=1.602\times10^{-19}\;\text{C}\label{eq:FundamentalCharge}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 we can covert eV to J:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
1\;\text{eV}=\frac{1\;\text{J}}{1\;\text{C}}\left(1.602\times10^{-19}\;\text{C}\right)=1.602\times10^{-19}\;\text{J}\label{eq:eV2J}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
The Matrix Elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The matrix elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 have certain meanings
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The diagonal elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 are denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{n|\hat{H}|n}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
These are called 
\series bold
occupation energies
\series default
 because they are the potential energy of the system when it occupies state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{n}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The off-diagonal elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 are denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{m|\hat{H}|n}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
These are called 
\series bold
transition energies
\series default
 because they are kinetic eneriges for a transition of the system from state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{m}$
\end_inset

 to state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{n}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
