\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
Exercise 3.9
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Consider the computational molecule of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:TwoDotStates"

\end_inset

.
 Write the Hamiltonian for this two-state molecule given that state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

 is an energy 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 higher than state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

,
 and the transition energy for 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}\rightarrow\ket{1}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}\rightarrow\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $-\gamma$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename img/qca-two-state-system.png
	width 30text%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The two states of a computational molecule.
 Black circles represent quantum dots,
 which trap a mobile charge.
 Two dots are separated by length 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

,
 and the connecting bar denotes a tunneling path between the dots.
 A red disc represents a mobile electron.
 The system has two classical states,
 which encode a bit.
 Fixed neutralizing charge present on each dot is represented as green filling in the dot.
 This indicates the presence of 
\begin_inset Formula $+q_{e}/2$
\end_inset

 on each dot.
 This keeps the molecules overall neutral,
 and we will not explicitly illustrate the neutralizing charge henceforth.
 Thus,
 when a mobile electron occupies a dot,
 the net charge on the dot is 
\begin_inset Formula $-q_{e}/2$
\end_inset

.
 The other (unoccupied) dot has the fixed neutralizing charge uncovered,
 so its net charge is 
\begin_inset Formula $+q_{e}/2$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:TwoDotStates"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We first write the Hamiltonian in terms of its four matrix elements:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
H_{00} & H_{01}\\
H_{10} & H_{11}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\braket{0|\hat{H}|0} & \braket{0|\hat{H}|1}\\
\braket{1|\hat{H}|0} & \braket{1|\hat{H}|1}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

The problem statement encodes for us the following relationships:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\braket{1|\hat{H}|1}-\braket{0|\hat{H}|0}=\Delta,\label{eq:Detuning}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\braket{1|\hat{H}|0}=\braket{0|\hat{H}|1}=-\gamma
\]

\end_inset

Since the occupation energies 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{n|\hat{H}|n}$
\end_inset

 are potential energies,
 we get to pick the reference potential energy,
 i.e.,
 we can choose the value of zero potential energy.
 This is equivalent to choosing a ground node in the circuit.
 Let us define the zero to be the midway point between 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{1|\hat{H}|1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{0|\hat{H}|0}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
0\equiv\frac{1}{2}\left(\braket{1|\hat{H}|1}+\braket{0|\hat{H}|0}\right)\label{eq:ChooseQCAZero}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We can eliminate 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{0|\hat{H}|0}$
\end_inset

 using Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Detuning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{1|\hat{H}|1}-\Delta=\braket{0|\hat{H}|0}$
\end_inset

 so that Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:ChooseQCAZero"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
0\equiv\frac{1}{2}\left(\braket{1|\hat{H}|1}+\braket{1|\hat{H}|1}-\Delta\right)\quad\rightarrow\quad\braket{1|\hat{H}|1}=\frac{\Delta}{2}.
\]

\end_inset

Similarly,
 we can use Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Detuning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{1|\hat{H}|1}=\braket{0|\hat{H}|0}+\Delta$
\end_inset

,
 which gives us
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
0\equiv\frac{1}{2}\left(\braket{0|\hat{H}|0}+\Delta+\braket{0|\hat{H}|0}\right)\quad\rightarrow\quad\braket{0|\hat{H}|0}=-\frac{\Delta}{2}
\]

\end_inset

Thus,
 we can use Equations 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sigmax"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sigmayz"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 to write the Hamiltonian as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{H}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
-\frac{\Delta}{2} & -\gamma\\
-\gamma & \frac{\Delta}{2}
\end{array}\right]=-\gamma\sigma_{x}+\frac{\Delta}{2}\sigma_{z}\label{eq:DrivenCellHamiltonian-QMconvention}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here,
 we have used 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{x}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{z}$
\end_inset

 to write the Hamiltonian in a nicer form.
 Notice that we are using the quantum mechanics convention of Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sigmayz"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

