\begin_layout Subsection
The Ground State
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The physics of the real world dictate that there is a 
\series bold
lowest eigenenergy
\series default
 for a given physical system,
 and it is called the 
\series bold
ground state energy
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The ground state energy is indexed using either 
\begin_inset Formula $n=0$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

,
 depending on the context (different physical systems have different conventions)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
For a trapped particle in a box,
 we typically use 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{1}$
\end_inset

 as the ground state energy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For a particle trapped in a parabolic potential (quantum harmonic oscillator),
 we usually label the ground state energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

 associated with the ground state energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{1}$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\series bold
ground state
\series default
.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Greyedout
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+lyx2org_list_scope:
 detach
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+begin_note
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
Exercise 3.10
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Calculate the ground state of the molecule from Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:TwoDotStates"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 by hand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We can do this by finding the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

.
 To find the eigenvalues,
 use
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\det\left(\hat{H}-E_{n}\mathbf{1}\right)=0
\]

\end_inset

Using the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 we found,
 we write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
0 & =\det\left(\hat{H}-E_{n}\mathbf{1}\right)=\det\left(\left[\begin{array}{cc}
-\frac{\Delta}{2} & -\gamma\\
-\gamma & \frac{\Delta}{2}
\end{array}\right]-E_{n}\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0\\
0 & 1
\end{array}\right]\right)=\left|\begin{array}{cc}
-\frac{\Delta}{2}-E_{n} & -\gamma\\
-\gamma & \frac{\Delta}{2}-E_{n}
\end{array}\right|\\
 & =\left(-\frac{\Delta}{2}-E_{n}\right)\left(\frac{\Delta}{2}-E_{n}\right)-\gamma^{2}\\
 & =-\frac{\Delta^{2}}{4}+E_{n}^{2}-\gamma^{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{n}=\pm\sqrt{\frac{\Delta^{2}}{4}+\gamma^{2}}=\pm\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\Delta^{2}+4\gamma^{2}}
\]

\end_inset

The ground state energy,
 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 is the lower of these values,
 and the first excited state energy is higher:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
E_{1} & =-\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\Delta^{2}+4\gamma^{2}}\\
E_{2} & =\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\Delta^{2}+4\gamma^{2}}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Now that we have 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 we can use it in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:TISE"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 along with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 to find 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi_{1}}$
\end_inset

.
 Write the time-independent Schrödinger equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi_{1}}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\hat{H}\ket{\psi_{1}} & =E_{1}\ket{\psi_{1}}\nonumber \\
 & \downarrow\nonumber \\
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
-\frac{\Delta}{2} & -\gamma\\
-\gamma & \frac{\Delta}{2}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
a\\
b
\end{array}\right] & =-\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\Delta^{2}+4\gamma^{2}}\left[\begin{array}{c}
a\\
b
\end{array}\right]\nonumber \\
 & \downarrow\nonumber \\
-\frac{\Delta}{2}a-\gamma b & =-a\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\Delta^{2}+4\gamma^{2}}\label{eq:QCAEigVectRow01}\\
-\gamma a+\frac{\Delta}{2}b & =-b\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\Delta^{2}+4\gamma^{2}}\label{eq:QCAEigVectRow02}
\end{align}

\end_inset

If we take 
\begin_inset Formula $b=1$
\end_inset

,
 then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
a=\frac{\Delta+\sqrt{\Delta^{2}+4\gamma^{2}}}{2\gamma}\label{eq:QCATwoDotParameter_a}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\psi_{0}}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
a\\
b
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{c}
a\\
1
\end{array}\right]=a\ket{0}+\ket{1}.
\]

\end_inset

We need to normalize this:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ket{\psi_{0}^{\prime}}=\frac{\ket{\psi_{0}}}{\sqrt{\braket{\psi_{0}|\psi_{0}}}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{a^{2}+1}}\left[\begin{array}{c}
a\\
1
\end{array}\right]\label{eq:NormalizedGroundState}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We could write the normalized ground state out in greater detail by substituting 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:QCATwoDotParameter_a"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 into Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:NormalizedGroundState"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 but things would just get messier,
 so let's not.
 It can be shown that the probability of measuring 0 and 1 are as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
p\left(0\right) & =\frac{a^{2}}{a^{2}+1}=\frac{1}{2}\left(1+\frac{\Delta}{\sqrt{4\gamma^{2}+\Delta^{2}}}\right)\\
p\left(1\right) & =\frac{1}{a^{2}+1}=\frac{1}{2}\left(1-\frac{\Delta}{\sqrt{4\gamma^{2}+\Delta^{2}}}\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Recall that we are using the quantum mechanics convention of Equation 
