\series bold
Exercise 3.15
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Determine the cost of a bit flip,
 given a fully-polarized driver.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{\text{drv}}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
 Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Delta_Pdrv_explicit"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 gives us
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta\left(1\right)=\left(1-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)\frac{q_{e}^{2}}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}a}\left(1\right).
\]

\end_inset

Because all the physical parameters and geometric constants are positive and 
\begin_inset Formula $1-1/\sqrt{2}>0$
\end_inset

,
 it must be that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta>0$
\end_inset

.
 What does this mean?
 Recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 is the potential energy by which 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

 is higher than 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

.
 This indicates to us that state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 is the preferred state for the target cell is if 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{\text{drv}}=1$
\end_inset

,
 and state 1 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 higher than state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

.
 In other words,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 is the amount of energy which must be added to the system to force the target cell to take state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

On the other hand,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{\text{drv}}=-1$
\end_inset

,
 Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Delta_Pdrv_explicit"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 gives us
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta\left(-1\right)=-\left(1-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)\frac{q_{e}^{2}}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}a}.
\]

\end_inset

This means that 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 is energetically higher than 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\left(-1\right)$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{\text{drv}}=-1$
\end_inset

.
 We must still excite the target cell by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 in order to make it occupy the state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 under these circumstances.
 In either case,
 the energy required to flip the target cell is the same,
 and we can interpret it as the cost of a bit flip.
 This is an important energy in the study of QCA,
 and we call it the kink energy,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{k}=\left(1-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)\frac{q_{e}^{2}}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}a}.\label{eq:KinkEnergy}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We see here that two-dot QCA cells in the configuration of the driver-target system prefer to anti-align.
 If we were to make two adjacent cells align,
 this is called a kink,
 hence the term kink energy.
 For a cell with 
\begin_inset Formula $a=1\;\text{nm}$
\end_inset

,
 we calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{k}=421\;\text{meV}.$
\end_inset


